export const ANTI_SLOP = "No word or phrase can prove a text was AI-generated. The most defensible approach is to look for several signals clustering together, compare them with the author’s prior work, and treat automated-detector scores as leads—not verdicts. OpenAI explicitly says reliable detection of all AI-written text is impossible, and Turnitin withholds detailed scores below 20% because this range is more prone to false positives. [OpenAI](https://openai.com/index/new-ai-classifier-for-indicating-ai-written-text/), [Turnitin](https://guides.turnitin.com/hc/en-us/articles/22774058814093-Using-the-AI-Writing-Report)\n\n## **Words empirically associated with AI-assisted prose**\n\nA large recent analysis of 490,000 English web pages labels the following as “AI-typical vocabulary.” These are useful only when unusually frequent or concentrated—not when appearing once. [Pew Research Center](https://www.pewresearch.org/chart/key-words-and-punctuation-favored-by-ai-has-spiked-since-the-release-of-chatgpt/)\n\n* additionally  \n* align with / aligns with  \n* boasts  \n* bolstered  \n* crucial  \n* delve / delves / delving  \n* emphasizing  \n* enduring  \n* enhance  \n* essential  \n* fostering  \n* garner  \n* highlight  \n* interplay  \n* intricate  \n* key  \n* landscape  \n* meticulous  \n* perfectly  \n* pivotal  \n* showcase  \n* significant  \n* tapestry  \n* testament  \n* underscore  \n* valuable  \n* vibrant\n\nEspecially notable in academic and scientific writing:\n\n* delve / delving into  \n* intricate  \n* underscore  \n* meticulous  \n* showcase  \n* realm  \n* pivotal  \n* align / aligns with  \n* utilize / utilisation\n\n“Delve,” “intricate,” and “underscore” have been formally studied as lexically overrepresented in LLM-generated scientific writing—not merely as internet folklore. [ACL study](https://aclanthology.org/2025.coling-main.426/)\n\n## **Common AI-like phrases and templates**\n\nThese are useful as pattern clues when several occur in a short passage.\n\n**Generic opening and framing**\n\n* “In today’s rapidly evolving landscape…”  \n* “In the realm of…”  \n* “At its core…”  \n* “This article/post explores…”  \n* “Let’s delve into…”  \n* “It is important to note that…”  \n* “It is worth noting that…”  \n* “A key aspect to consider is…”  \n* “This underscores the importance of…”  \n* “This highlights the need for…”\n\n**Overly polished transitions**\n\n* “Moreover,”  \n* “Furthermore,”  \n* “Additionally,”  \n* “In addition,”  \n* “That said,”  \n* “With that in mind,”  \n* “As a result,”  \n* “Consequently,”  \n* “Ultimately,”  \n* “In conclusion,”\n\nResearch comparing prompted GPT writing with human writing found GPT used additive transitions—especially “moreover,” “furthermore,” “also,” and “in addition”—more often. [Modern Language Journal study](https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/modl.70089)\n\n**Broad, low-commitment claims**\n\n* “plays a crucial role”  \n* “has the potential to”  \n* “offers a wide range of benefits”  \n* “is essential for”  \n* “can be a valuable tool”  \n* “fosters innovation”  \n* “drives meaningful change”  \n* “creates a more inclusive / sustainable future”  \n* “is a testament to”  \n* “continues to shape the landscape of”\n\n**Balanced, formulaic contrast**\n\n* “It is not just X; it is Y.”  \n* “While X is important, Y is equally important.”  \n* “On the one hand … on the other hand …”  \n* “Whether you are X or Y…”  \n* “From X to Y…”  \n* “By doing so, …”  \n* “Not only X, but also Y.”\n\n**List-and-wrap-up endings**\n\n* “Here are the key takeaways:”  \n* “In summary, …”  \n* “To sum up, …”  \n* “Ultimately, the key is to…”  \n* “By understanding these factors, you can…”  \n* “The future looks promising.”\n\n## **Structural signals that matter more than a word list**\n\nLook for a cluster of these:\n\n* Uniformly polished grammar, sentence length, and paragraph rhythm.  \n* A predictable essay arc: broad introduction, three tidy points, generic conclusion.  \n* Repeated transitions at the start of nearly every paragraph.  \n* Many abstract claims but few concrete, verifiable details, lived experiences, or source-specific observations.  \n* Symmetrical “pros and cons” treatment even where a real author would likely take a clear position.  \n* Excessive restatement of the prompt or conclusion.  \n* Safe, agreeable tone; weak authorial stance; few genuine uncertainties.  \n* Definitions and explanations that are technically fluent but shallow or noncommittal.  \n* Generic examples that could fit almost any topic.  \n* Overuse of headings, bullets, “Key takeaways,” FAQs, or explanatory asides when the requested format did not call for them.  \n* Frequent em dashes, parenthetical qualifiers, and long appositive phrases—again, only as supporting evidence.\n\nStylometry research finds that detection works best from combinations of lexical, grammatical, syntactic, punctuation, and distributional features—not individual “AI words.” [Stylometry study](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0957417425026181)\n\n## **A practical evidence scale**\n\n| Signal | How to interpret it |\n| ----- | ----- |\n| One “AI word,” e.g. *delve* | No meaningful evidence |\n| Several watchwords in a short passage | Weak clue |\n| Watchwords \\+ formulaic transitions \\+ generic claims | Moderate reason to inspect |\n| Pattern cluster \\+ sharp shift from the author’s established voice | Stronger concern |\n| Pattern cluster \\+ no drafts, sources, notes, or ability to explain choices | Basis for a conversation, not automatic punishment |\n\nA careful reviewer should preserve drafts and revision history, ask the author to explain sources and choices, compare with known writing, and verify claims/citations. Do not accuse someone based on vocabulary alone—these words are increasingly entering ordinary human writing, which makes lexical “tells” less reliable over time. [ACL coevolution research](https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-acl.657/)\n\nWrite in a clear, natural, human voice. Prioritize specificity, honest judgment, and useful detail over polished-sounding generalities.\n\nAvoid AI-associated filler and elevated vocabulary unless it is genuinely the most precise word. In particular, avoid or use sparingly: “delve,” “tapestry,” “landscape,” “realm,” “intricate,” “pivotal,” “crucial,” “underscore,” “foster,” “showcase,” “vibrant,” “testament,” “meticulous,” “seamless,” “multifaceted,” “transformative,” “leverage,” “navigate,” and “unlock.”\n\nAvoid stock phrases and replace them with direct statements:\n\n* Do not write “In today’s rapidly evolving landscape.” Start with the actual subject.  \n* Do not write “It is important to note.” State the important point.  \n* Do not write “This underscores/highlights the importance of.” Explain the consequence.  \n* Do not write “plays a crucial role.” Say what it does.  \n* Do not write “has the potential to.” State the likely outcome and its limits.  \n* Do not write “a wide range of benefits.” Name the two or three actual benefits.  \n* Do not write “in the realm of.” Use “in,” “for,” or name the field.  \n* Do not write “let’s delve into.” Begin the explanation.  \n* Do not write “In conclusion” or “Ultimately.” End with the conclusion itself.\n\nUse transitions only when they genuinely show a relationship. Avoid mechanical chains of “Moreover,” “Furthermore,” “Additionally,” and “Consequently.” Prefer plain links such as “But,” “So,” “For example,” “At the same time,” or no transition at all.\n\nDo not force every paragraph into a tidy structure. Vary paragraph length according to the idea. Some points can be handled in one blunt sentence; others may need a short example or qualification. Avoid three-item lists merely because they sound balanced.\n\nUse concrete evidence:\n\n* Name people, places, dates, tools, decisions, examples, and constraints where relevant.  \n* Explain why a point matters in this situation, rather than making a broad claim that fits any topic.  \n* Include a real limitation, tradeoff, doubt, or exception when one exists.  \n* Prefer an example from the actual context over a generic hypothetical.\n\nSound like a person with a viewpoint:\n\n* Make a defensible judgment instead of presenting every issue as perfectly balanced.  \n* Use restrained first person when appropriate: “I think,” “I found,” “In my experience,” or “My concern is.”  \n* Do not manufacture personal experiences or uncertainty. Only include what is true.  \n* Let minor variations in rhythm remain. Do not polish every sentence into the same length or cadence.\n\nKeep grammar and facts accurate. “Natural” does not mean adding misspellings, bad grammar, fake anecdotes, random slang, or deliberate mistakes. It means removing generic filler, preserving genuine voice, and allowing writing to be specific rather than uniformly polished.\n\nBefore finalizing, check:\n\n1. Does every paragraph add a new, concrete idea?  \n2. Could a reader tell what I actually think or recommend?  \n3. Have I replaced broad praise with evidence?  \n4. Have I removed unnecessary headings, summaries, and repeated conclusions?  \n5. Does this sound like the author’s normal vocabulary and level of formality?\n\nDo not write to sound impressive. Write to be understood.\n\nDo not use inflated, vague, or fashionable language when a plain word is stronger.\n\nDo not use “delve,” “tapestry,” “realm,” “landscape,” “intricate,” “pivotal,” “crucial,” “multifaceted,” “transformative,” “vibrant,” “seamless,” “meticulous,” “robust,” “foster,” “leverage,” “navigate,” “underscore,” “showcase,” “unlock,” “garner,” “bolster,” or “utilize” unless the word is necessary and more exact than a simple alternative.\n\nDo not begin with empty scene-setting such as:\n\n* “In today’s fast-paced world…”  \n* “In an ever-evolving landscape…”  \n* “In the modern era…”  \n* “In the realm of…”  \n* “As we navigate…”  \n* “It is important to note…”  \n* “It is worth noting…”  \n* “At its core…”  \n* “When it comes to…”  \n* “In recent years…”\n\nDo not announce what the writing will do. Do not write “This article will explore,” “Let’s delve into,” “In this post, we will discuss,” or “This essay aims to highlight.” Begin with the point itself.\n\nDo not use fake emphasis. Do not write “very,” “really,” “extremely,” “incredibly,” “highly,” “truly,” “undeniably,” or “without a doubt” unless evidence supports that intensity.\n\nDo not write generic importance claims:\n\n* “plays a crucial role”  \n* “is essential”  \n* “is a key factor”  \n* “has significant implications”  \n* “offers a wide range of benefits”  \n* “can make a meaningful difference”  \n* “has the potential to transform”  \n* “is a testament to”  \n* “highlights the importance of”\n\nInstead, state what changes, for whom, under what conditions, and why.\n\nDo not pile up transitions. Do not begin every paragraph with “Moreover,” “Furthermore,” “Additionally,” “Consequently,” “Therefore,” “In addition,” or “On the other hand.” Use a transition only if it reveals a real connection. Often, no transition is better.\n\nDo not force a balanced argument when the evidence is not balanced. Do not use “On the one hand … on the other hand …” just to sound fair. State the strongest case, then explain the real limitation or objection.\n\nDo not use formulaic contrast constructions repeatedly:\n\n* “It is not just X; it is Y.”  \n* “Not only X, but also Y.”  \n* “Whether you are X or Y…”  \n* “From X to Y…”  \n* “More than just X…”  \n* “X is not merely Y—it is Z.”\n\nUse them only when the contrast is genuinely useful.\n\nDo not make claims that could apply to any subject. If a sentence could be pasted into an article about education, marketing, health, technology, or leadership without changing it, rewrite it with specific details.\n\nDo not replace evidence with praise. Do not call something “innovative,” “powerful,” “valuable,” “effective,” “comprehensive,” or “groundbreaking” without explaining exactly what it does and how that judgment is supported.\n\nDo not use long lists of adjectives. Do not write “a dynamic, innovative, inclusive, sustainable, and forward-thinking approach.” Name the one or two qualities that matter, then show them.\n\nDo not repeat the same conclusion in the introduction, body, summary, and final paragraph. Say it once well. Each paragraph must add information, evidence, interpretation, or a useful qualification.\n\nDo not add a summary merely because the text is ending. Do not automatically write “In conclusion,” “To sum up,” “In summary,” “Ultimately,” or “The key takeaway is.” End when the final point has been made.\n\nDo not over-explain obvious points. Do not define common terms unless the audience needs the definition. Do not turn every answer into a beginner’s guide.\n\nDo not rely on generic examples. Do not invent a hypothetical scenario when a real example, source, detail, or concrete consequence is available.\n\nDo not use statistics, citations, names, quotations, dates, or technical claims unless they can be verified. Do not create placeholder references or vague attributions such as “studies show” or “experts agree.”\n\nDo not pretend certainty. Do not hide limitations, disagreement, uncertainty, tradeoffs, or missing evidence. State what is known, what is inferred, and what remains unclear.\n\nDo not write in a uniformly polished rhythm. Do not make every sentence the same length or structure. Mix short direct sentences with longer explanatory ones when the idea requires it.\n\nDo not make every paragraph the same size. A sharp observation may need one sentence. A difficult idea may need a fuller explanation. Let the material determine the shape.\n\nDo not use an em dash in every paragraph. Do not use parentheses to add information that belongs in the sentence. Do not use semicolons merely to sound formal.\n\nDo not make a list when a sentence would be clearer. Do not add headings, subheadings, “Key Takeaways,” FAQs, or bullet points unless they improve navigation for the reader.\n\nDo not overuse rhetorical questions. Do not ask a question only to answer it immediately. State the point directly.\n\nDo not use empty reassurance or sales language:\n\n* “The good news is…”  \n* “The possibilities are endless.”  \n* “The future is bright.”  \n* “This exciting opportunity…”  \n* “A game-changer.”  \n* “A win-win.”  \n* “A new era.”  \n* “The next level.”\n\nDo not sound like a brochure, press release, consultant deck, or automated help page unless that tone is specifically required.\n\nDo not imitate another person’s voice. Do not manufacture personal stories, emotions, doubts, mistakes, or experiences. Do not add intentional grammar errors, misspellings, slang, or random imperfections. Natural writing is not careless writing.\n\nDo not remove every sign of personality. Keep genuine preferences, direct judgments, precise observations, and honest uncertainty when they belong.\n\nDo not use words that the writer would never normally use. Match the author’s real vocabulary, level of formality, and sentence rhythm.\n\nDo not submit writing that the author cannot explain, defend, revise, or trace back to their own notes and sources.\n\nBefore finalizing, remove every sentence that does not do at least one of these things: provide a fact, give an example, explain a reason, make a supported judgment, address a limitation, or move the reader to the next necessary idea.\n\n";
export const MY_TERMS = "# My Terms\n\nThis is the core proprietary vocabulary for my content.\n\nThese terms should appear repeatedly across videos, captions, carousels,\nlong-form content, and product narratives so the audience gradually\nassociates the concepts with my brand.\n\nThe goal is not to sound complicated. The goal is to create **memorable\nlanguage for ideas I repeatedly teach**.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 1. Time-Maxing\n\n**Meaning:** Intentionally getting more meaningful value out of the\nlimited time available to you.\n\nTime-maxing is not simply about doing more work. It means using your\ntime deliberately across the things that matter: meaningful work,\nlearning, health, relationships, hobbies, experiences, and rest.\n\n**Core idea:**\n\n> You do not need more than 24 hours. You need to use your 24 hours\n> better.\n\n**Example script line:**\n\n> \"I don't believe in working every waking hour. I believe in\n> Time-Maxing.\"\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 2. Attention Budget\n\n**Meaning:** Treating attention as a limited resource that should be\ndeliberately allocated.\n\nJust as money has a budget, attention should have one too.\n\nThe question is not only:\n\n> \"How much time do I have?\"\n\nIt is also:\n\n> \"Where am I spending my limited attention?\"\n\n**Example script line:**\n\n> \"Your time budget might be 24 hours, but your attention budget is much\n> smaller.\"\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 3. Attention Tax\n\n**Meaning:** The hidden cognitive cost created by distractions,\nnotifications, context switching, and constantly interrupted attention.\n\nThe visible distraction may last only a few minutes, while the loss of\nfocus can affect the quality of the work that follows.\n\n**Example script line:**\n\n> \"The problem with your phone isn't only the 20 minutes you lose. It's\n> the Attention Tax you pay afterward.\"\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 4. AI Leverage\n\n**Meaning:** Using AI to multiply what you can accomplish rather than\nusing it merely as a shortcut.\n\nAI leverage means giving AI appropriate parts of the workflow while\nretaining human judgment, direction, verification, creativity, and\ndecision-making.\n\n**Example script line:**\n\n> \"The future isn't about working harder. It's about learning how to use\n> AI leverage.\"\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 5. AI Amplification\n\n**Meaning:** Using AI to strengthen an existing human capability instead\nof replacing that capability entirely.\n\nAI should amplify thinking, research, writing, analysis,\nexperimentation, and execution.\n\nIt should not become an excuse to stop thinking independently.\n\n**Example script line:**\n\n> \"Use AI for amplification, not intellectual outsourcing.\"\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 6. Friction Design\n\n**Meaning:** Deliberately increasing friction around behaviours you want\nto reduce and decreasing friction around behaviours you want to\nencourage.\n\nExamples:\n\n-   Block distracting apps.\n-   Put the phone away before deep work.\n-   Keep gym clothes ready.\n-   Prepare study materials in advance.\n-   Remove unnecessary steps from important routines.\n\n**Core idea:**\n\n> Make the right behaviour easier and the wrong behaviour harder.\n\n**Example script line:**\n\n> \"You don't always need more discipline. Sometimes you need better\n> Friction Design.\"\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 7. Discipline Debt\n\n**Meaning:** The accumulated consequences of repeatedly postponing\nbehaviours you know matter.\n\nSmall acts of avoidance can accumulate.\n\nExamples include repeatedly skipping exercise, postponing important\nstudy, avoiding difficult tasks, or continuously choosing distraction\nover deliberate action.\n\n**Example script line:**\n\n> \"Every time you keep postponing something important, you may be adding\n> to your Discipline Debt.\"\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 8. Skill Stacking\n\n**Meaning:** Developing multiple complementary skills so that they\nreinforce one another.\n\nInstead of relying on a single capability, build combinations.\n\nFor example:\n\n> Medicine + research + communication + AI + writing\n\nThe combination can create a distinctive capability profile.\n\n**Example script line:**\n\n> \"You don't need to be the best in the world at one thing. Start Skill\n> Stacking.\"\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 9. Polymath Stack\n\n**Meaning:** Your personal combination of knowledge and skills across\nmultiple domains.\n\nThe Polymath Stack is the broader identity-level version of Skill\nStacking.\n\nIt represents the deliberate construction of a multidisciplinary\ncapability set.\n\n**Example script line:**\n\n> \"Your degree is one layer of your identity. Your Polymath Stack is\n> everything else you choose to build.\"\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 10. Ambition Gap\n\n**Meaning:** The distance between the life you currently live and the\nlife you believe you could build.\n\nThe Ambition Gap can come from:\n\n-   lack of skills\n-   poor execution\n-   distraction\n-   inconsistent habits\n-   fear of failure\n-   comfort\n-   lack of clarity\n\n**Example script line:**\n\n> \"Your problem might not be a lack of ambition. It might be the size of\n> your Ambition Gap.\"\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 11. Comfort Tax\n\n**Meaning:** The future cost of repeatedly choosing short-term comfort\nover long-term growth.\n\nComfort can feel cheap today while becoming expensive later.\n\nExamples:\n\n-   avoiding difficult learning\n-   choosing scrolling instead of important work\n-   refusing to leave the comfort zone\n-   consistently choosing convenience over growth\n\n**Example script line:**\n\n> \"Comfort feels free today, but you might be paying a Comfort Tax years\n> from now.\"\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 12. Energy-Maxxing\n\n**Meaning:** Deliberately managing physical, mental, and emotional\nenergy so that your best energy is directed toward your highest-value\nactivities.\n\nTime management asks:\n\n> \"Where is my time going?\"\n\nEnergy-maxxing asks:\n\n> \"Where is my best energy going?\"\n\nIt recognizes that two hours of highly focused, high-energy work are not\nnecessarily equivalent to two hours performed while exhausted and\ndistracted.\n\n**Example script line:**\n\n> \"I care more about Energy-Maxxing than squeezing another two hours\n> into my day.\"\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# How to Use These Terms in Scripts\n\n## 1. Build the vocabulary around recurring problems\n\nDo not force a term into every video.\n\nInstead, connect each concept to the problem it explains.\n\n### Time problem\n\nUse:\n\n**Time-Maxing**\n\n### Distraction problem\n\nUse:\n\n**Attention Budget → Attention Tax**\n\n### AI workflow problem\n\nUse:\n\n**AI Leverage → AI Amplification**\n\n### Habit/discipline problem\n\nUse:\n\n**Friction Design → Discipline Debt**\n\n### Career/learning problem\n\nUse:\n\n**Skill Stacking → Polymath Stack**\n\n### Ambition problem\n\nUse:\n\n**Ambition Gap → Comfort Tax**\n\n### Energy problem\n\nUse:\n\n**Energy-Maxxing**\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 2. Define a new term the first few times you use it\n\nWhen introducing a concept, do not assume the audience already\nunderstands it.\n\nUse a simple structure:\n\n> **Term → simple definition → example → takeaway**\n\nExample:\n\n> \"I call this the Attention Tax. It's the hidden cost you pay after\n> every distraction. You check Instagram for ten minutes, but the real\n> cost is the focus you lose when you return to work.\"\n\nOnce the audience understands the term, you can use it without\nexplaining it every time.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 3. Use the terms as hooks\n\nYour proprietary vocabulary should help create recognizable hooks.\n\nExamples:\n\n> \"You're not bad at time management. You're bad at Time-Maxing.\"\n\n> \"You don't have an attention problem. You have an Attention Budget\n> problem.\"\n\n> \"The hidden cost of your phone is the Attention Tax.\"\n\n> \"Stop asking AI to do the work. Start building AI Leverage.\"\n\n> \"Your environment is stronger than your willpower. Learn Friction\n> Design.\"\n\n> \"Every ignored responsibility creates Discipline Debt.\"\n\n> \"Stop collecting random hobbies. Build a Polymath Stack.\"\n\n> \"Your biggest problem might be the Ambition Gap.\"\n\n> \"Comfort isn't free. It comes with a Comfort Tax.\"\n\n> \"Stop optimizing your schedule and start Energy-Maxxing.\"\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 4. Turn individual terms into recurring series\n\nA term becomes powerful when the audience sees it repeatedly.\n\nPossible recurring series:\n\n### Time-Maxing\n\n-   \"3 Time-Maxing rules\"\n-   \"Time-Maxing my week\"\n-   \"Time-Maxing as a medical student\"\n-   \"Time-Maxing without working 14 hours\"\n\n### Attention Budget / Attention Tax\n\n-   \"Protect your Attention Budget\"\n-   \"Where your Attention Tax is coming from\"\n-   \"How I reduced my Attention Tax\"\n\n### AI Leverage / AI Amplification\n\n-   \"AI Leverage for students\"\n-   \"How I use AI to amplify my workflow\"\n-   \"AI Leverage instead of AI dependence\"\n\n### Friction Design / Discipline Debt\n\n-   \"Friction Design 101\"\n-   \"How I use friction to beat distraction\"\n-   \"Are you accumulating Discipline Debt?\"\n\n### Skill Stacking / Polymath Stack\n\n-   \"Build your Polymath Stack\"\n-   \"My current Skill Stack\"\n-   \"Why ambitious students should Skill Stack\"\n\n### Ambition Gap / Comfort Tax\n\n-   \"Close your Ambition Gap\"\n-   \"The hidden Comfort Tax\"\n-   \"Comfort vs ambition\"\n\n### Energy-Maxxing\n\n-   \"Energy-Maxxing my study day\"\n-   \"Why Energy-Maxxing beats time management\"\n-   \"Protect your highest-energy hours\"\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 5. Create contrasts\n\nContrasts make these concepts easier to remember.\n\nExamples:\n\n> **Time management vs Time-Maxing**\n\n> **Willpower vs Friction Design**\n\n> **Screen time vs Attention Tax**\n\n> **AI dependence vs AI Leverage**\n\n> **Random skills vs a Polymath Stack**\n\n> **Short-term comfort vs Comfort Tax**\n\n> **More hours vs Energy-Maxxing**\n\nThese comparisons can become entire short-form videos.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 6. Make the terminology feel natural\n\nThe goal is not to say:\n\n> \"Here is another proprietary framework I have invented.\"\n\nInstead, speak as though these are natural concepts in your worldview.\n\nFor example:\n\n> \"I've started thinking about productivity less as time management and\n> more as Time-Maxing.\"\n\nThat feels conversational.\n\nThen repeat the term across future content until the audience begins\nusing it themselves.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 7. Do not overload a single script\n\nUse **one primary proprietary term per short-form video** whenever\npossible.\n\nA second term can support it, but too many coined concepts at once will\nmake the message feel artificial.\n\nA useful structure:\n\n**Hook** → **one core term** → **explanation** → **real example** →\n**action** → **CTA**\n\nExample:\n\n> \"Your phone isn't stealing just your time. It's charging you an\n> Attention Tax.\"\n>\n> Explain the concept.\n>\n> Show a real example.\n>\n> Give one practical intervention.\n>\n> \"That's why I use Focalyst to add friction between me and distracting\n> apps.\"\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 8. Connect the vocabulary to Focalyst naturally\n\nFocalyst should become the practical implementation layer of these\nideas.\n\nExamples:\n\n**Time-Maxing** → planning\n\n**Attention Budget** → deciding what deserves attention\n\n**Attention Tax** → identifying distraction\n\n**Friction Design** → app blocking\n\n**Energy-Maxxing** → scheduling demanding work around energy\n\n**Discipline Debt** → tracking consistency\n\nThe product should demonstrate the philosophy rather than interrupting\nthe educational content with an unrelated advertisement.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# The vocabulary flywheel\n\nThe long-term goal is:\n\n**Coin the concept** → **Define it** → **Teach it** → **Use it\nrepeatedly** → **Show it in your own life** → **Turn it into a\nframework** → **Attach it to a product or resource** → **Let the\naudience start using the term**\n\nThe strongest sign that a term has become associated with the brand is\nwhen people start using it without being prompted.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# Core vocabulary at a glance\n\n  -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n  Term                                Meaning\n  ----------------------------------- -----------------------------------\n  **Time-Maxing**                     Getting more meaningful value from\n                                      limited time\n\n  **Attention Budget**                Deliberately allocating limited\n                                      attention\n\n  **Attention Tax**                   Hidden cost of distraction and\n                                      fragmented attention\n\n  **AI Leverage**                     Using AI to multiply capability\n\n  **AI Amplification**                Using AI to strengthen human\n                                      capability\n\n  **Friction Design**                 Shaping behaviour by\n                                      adding/removing friction\n\n  **Discipline Debt**                 Accumulated cost of repeatedly\n                                      avoiding important actions\n\n  **Skill Stacking**                  Combining complementary skills\n\n  **Polymath Stack**                  Your personal multidisciplinary\n                                      capability profile\n\n  **Ambition Gap**                    Distance between current life and\n                                      desired potential\n\n  **Comfort Tax**                     Future cost of repeatedly choosing\n                                      short-term comfort\n\n  **Energy-Maxxing**                  Directing your best energy toward\n                                      your highest-value activities\n  -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## The overarching philosophy\n\nThese terms should reinforce one larger message:\n\n> **Own your time. Protect your attention. Manage your energy. Use AI as\n> leverage. Build discipline through systems. Stack skills. Think\n> bigger.**\n\nThat is the vocabulary system behind the brand.\n";
export const MY_TAKES = "# My Takes\n\n## Core worldview\n\nI believe ambitious people should build lives around **agency,\ndeliberate effort, curiosity, and control of attention**.\n\nMy philosophy is not about becoming a productivity machine. It is about\nbecoming more capable while still having a life worth living.\n\nI believe we should think bigger than what currently seems realistic,\nbut then become disciplined enough to turn that ambition into action.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# 1. I do not believe in multitasking\n\nI think humans often fool themselves into believing that multitasking\nsaves time.\n\nIn most demanding cognitive work, we cannot give our full attention to\nseveral things simultaneously. When we keep switching between important\ntasks:\n\n-   quality drops\n-   concentration is fragmented\n-   work becomes less satisfying\n-   supposed time savings can be misleading\n\nMy default principle is:\n\n> **One important thing at a time.**\n\nThere is an important distinction, however.\n\nSome forms of multitasking can work when one activity requires\nsubstantial cognitive processing while the other is largely habitual,\nautomatic, or reflexive.\n\nFor example, an activity that requires analytical attention can\npotentially be paired with a habitual activity that does not demand much\ndecision-making.\n\nThe problem is not simply \"doing two physical things at once.\" The\nproblem is asking the brain to perform multiple demanding cognitive\noperations at the same time.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# 2. Dream ridiculously --- then ground the dream in reality\n\nI believe in **positive thinking, extreme self-belief, and ambitious\ndreams**.\n\nI am comfortable with the idea of being \"delusional\" when that means\nhaving the courage to pursue something that currently looks like a\ndaydream.\n\nBut dreaming alone accomplishes nothing.\n\nMy philosophy is:\n\n> **Dream without artificially limiting yourself. Then use reality to\n> determine the strategy.**\n\nAmbition should be paired with execution.\n\nBuilding a strategy is often the easy part. The difficult part is:\n\n-   staying consistent\n-   doing the work\n-   implementing the strategy\n-   adapting when reality disagrees with the original plan\n\nThe dream gives direction. Reality gives feedback. Consistent execution\ndetermines how far we actually go.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# 3. Extreme self-belief protects curiosity\n\nI think many people spend years seeking external validation.\n\nEventually, they become afraid of looking foolish, failing publicly, or\ntrying something unconventional. They quietly kill their own curiosity\nbefore discovering what they might have been capable of.\n\nI want younger people to believe in themselves enough to:\n\n-   pursue bold ideas\n-   challenge themselves\n-   try things they have never tried before\n-   take meaningful risks\n-   pursue breakthroughs\n-   remain curious\n\nI believe that when a problem exists, we should at least ask:\n\n> **What if there is a solution?**\n\nFor things that already exist, we can use evidence to determine what has\nbeen achieved and what approaches have worked.\n\nFor genuinely novel problems, there may be no previous roadmap. That is\nwhere extreme self-belief and determination become especially important.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# 4. Delusion needs evidence when evidence exists\n\nI do not believe that self-belief means ignoring reality.\n\nWhen something has already been demonstrated, we can use evidence to\ndetermine:\n\n-   whether it is possible\n-   what has already been achieved\n-   what constraints exist\n-   which strategies have worked\n-   what needs to change\n\nBut when pursuing something genuinely novel, there may be no existing\nevidence that tells us exactly how to succeed.\n\nIn those situations, someone has to be willing to believe before the\nevidence exists.\n\n> **Evidence tells us what has been demonstrated. Imagination allows us\n> to attempt what has not yet been demonstrated.**\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# 5. Failure is part of the process\n\nI do not measure a meaningful pursuit only by its final outcome.\n\nIf someone dedicates years to a difficult goal and genuinely does\neverything within their capabilities to pursue it, those years can still\nbe extraordinarily valuable even if the final outcome is failure.\n\nFailure teaches us how to become successful faster than success itself\ncan.\n\nFailure can reveal:\n\n-   what does not work\n-   where our assumptions were wrong\n-   what skills we lack\n-   what constraints we underestimated\n-   how we need to adapt\n\nThe goal is not to eliminate failure.\n\nThe goal is to make failure **informative**.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# 6. Mediocrity is often self-imposed\n\nI see mediocrity as more than simply achieving average results.\n\nTo me, mediocrity can come from:\n\n-   lack of discipline\n-   lack of ambition\n-   killing one's potential before reaching its peak\n-   choosing short-term comfort over long-term growth\n-   constantly seeking validation\n-   becoming dependent on shortcuts and hacks\n-   refusing to leave the comfort zone\n\nThe dangerous part is not being average at something today.\n\nThe dangerous part is deciding that comfort is more important than\ndiscovering what you are capable of becoming.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# 7. Take calculated risks outside the comfort zone\n\nWhen I talk about taking risks, I do not mean reckless behaviour.\n\nI mean being willing to take on difficult, unfamiliar challenges even\nwhen they are inconvenient.\n\nFor example:\n\n-   learning a new language while pursuing a demanding professional\n    degree\n-   learning an instrument\n-   taking leadership responsibility\n-   developing a new skill outside one's primary field\n-   attempting to compete with people who have been doing something for\n    years\n\nThese choices require guts because there is a real possibility of\nlooking inexperienced.\n\nBut that is exactly where growth can happen.\n\nI believe we often underestimate what we can develop through deliberate\ntraining and sustained effort.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# 8. I believe strongly in neuroplasticity\n\nI believe the brain is capable of learning and adapting.\n\nI therefore believe that abilities that initially appear far outside our\ncurrent capability can sometimes be developed through deliberate\npractice, appropriate training, repetition, and sustained effort.\n\nI do not want people to assume that their current level of ability is\ntheir permanent ceiling.\n\n> **Current ability is a starting point, not necessarily a final\n> limit.**\n\nExtraordinary results can include academic achievement, physical\ncapability, intellectual development, creative ability,\nentrepreneurship, technical skills, leadership, and learning across\nmultiple disciplines.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# 9. Technology should be an enabler, not an owner\n\nI do not believe technology itself is the enemy.\n\nWe live in a connected world, and social media and technology can\nprovide enormous value.\n\nThe problem is losing control over how we use them.\n\nMy principle is:\n\n> **Use your phone. Your phone should not use you.**\n\nTechnology should expand human capability rather than continuously\nconsume human attention.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# 10. We cannot beat attention algorithms through willpower alone\n\nModern digital platforms are extremely effective at capturing attention.\n\nI do not think humans should assume that willpower alone is enough to\ncompete with increasingly sophisticated attention-optimization systems.\n\nWe need **friction and environmental constraints**.\n\nThat can include:\n\n-   app blockers\n-   notification limits\n-   removing unnecessary apps\n-   tracking screen time\n-   creating physical distance from the phone\n-   deliberately designing the digital environment\n\nThis is not weakness.\n\n> **Designing your environment is itself a form of discipline.**\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# 11. Focalyst is about ownership of time\n\nFocalyst should represent more than a collection of productivity\nfeatures.\n\nIts core idea is:\n\n> **Own Your Time.**\n\nI see Focalyst as:\n\n> **A personal operating system that helps you take control of your\n> life.**\n\nThe app blocker is particularly important within this philosophy.\n\nIf social media and \"brain-rot\" apps are consuming valuable time,\ncreating an obstacle between the person and the app can make intentional\nbehaviour easier.\n\nThe goal is not to make people dependent on Focalyst forever.\n\nThe goal is to help them regain control over their attention and build\nbehaviours that eventually become more automatic.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# 12. Responsibility for digital distraction is shared\n\nIf someone spends six hours a day scrolling, I do not think\nresponsibility belongs entirely to the individual.\n\nMy current way of thinking about it is roughly:\n\n-   **50% environment**\n-   **30% platform**\n-   **20% individual**\n\nThe exact percentages are less important than the principle: behaviour\nis shaped by the environment in which it occurs.\n\nThe individual still has responsibility.\n\nThey should:\n\n-   track screen time\n-   understand their patterns\n-   create constraints\n-   use tools such as app blockers\n-   deliberately redesign their environment\n\nBut we should not pretend that a human brain is operating in a neutral\nenvironment.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# 13. Productivity means control, not maximum output\n\nI do not define productivity as doing the maximum possible amount of\nwork.\n\nI define it as:\n\n> **Having greater control over your time so you can spend it on the\n> things that actually matter.**\n\nThat includes:\n\n-   meaningful work\n-   learning\n-   health\n-   relationships\n-   family\n-   friendships\n-   experiences\n-   exploration\n-   rest\n\nA person who completes 14 hours of work but has no control over their\nlife is not necessarily more productive than someone who completes six\nfocused hours and uses the remaining time intentionally.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# 14. Extreme ambition should not become obsession\n\nI believe extreme ambition can provide motivation without requiring\nconstant motivational content.\n\nWhen the goal itself matters deeply, you should not need to watch\nanother motivational video every morning to convince yourself to work.\n\nBut ambition should not become destructive obsession.\n\nMany pursuits have delayed rewards. You can work extremely hard for\nmonths without immediately seeing money, recognition, academic results,\nbusiness growth, or visible transformation.\n\nIf someone becomes obsessed while constantly expecting immediate\nrewards, the gap between effort and reward can create frustration.\n\nThe pursuit needs meaning beyond instant gratification.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# 15. I reject the \"5 AM or you are failing\" philosophy\n\nI do not believe waking up at 5 AM is a universal requirement for\nsuccess.\n\nPeople need sufficient sleep.\n\nMy current view is that roughly **6--7 hours of sleep** should be\ntreated as a practical minimum target, rather than glorifying sleep\ndeprivation.\n\nI also reject the idea that an ambitious person should lock themselves\naway and work on one project at the expense of everything else.\n\nHumans are social animals.\n\nWe need:\n\n-   friends\n-   family\n-   relationships\n-   experiences\n-   physical activity\n-   new environments\n-   both good and difficult experiences\n\nYou do not know how long you will live.\n\n> **Do not become so focused on building a future that you forget to\n> live the present.**\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# 16. Do not wait for the perfect moment\n\nI do not believe meaningful change needs to begin on January 1, Monday,\nthe next semester, or after life becomes less busy.\n\nIf something matters, start.\n\nThe ideal person does not wait for a new year to begin something\nmeaningful.\n\nThey act when they recognize the need for change.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# 17. Track behaviour until it becomes automatic\n\nI believe tracking is particularly useful when building new behaviours.\n\nA person can deliberately track activities such as:\n\n-   gym sessions\n-   swimming\n-   study blocks\n-   habits\n-   screen time\n-   focused work\n\nThe purpose is not to track everything forever.\n\nThe purpose is to make the behaviour visible until repetition makes it\nincreasingly automatic.\n\nA useful example is brushing your teeth. You normally do not need a\nmotivational speech or detailed reminder to do it. It has become part of\nthe automatic structure of daily life.\n\nThe objective of habit systems is to move useful behaviours in that\ndirection.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# 18. AI is an amplifier, not a replacement for thinking\n\nI do not believe people should be afraid of AI.\n\nWe should adopt it where it improves efficiency and capability.\n\nBut there is an important boundary:\n\n> **Use AI to increase your capabilities, not to outsource your ability\n> to think.**\n\nOver-reliance on AI can weaken the very abilities we are trying to\ndevelop.\n\nPeople should continue to:\n\n-   think independently\n-   question outputs\n-   solve problems\n-   write\n-   create\n-   reason\n-   make decisions\n\nAI should be an amplifier of human intelligence, not a substitute for\nit.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# 19. A meaningful life is bigger than productivity\n\nI do not want productivity to become another form of self-imposed\nimprisonment.\n\nThe objective is not:\n\n> Work more → optimize more → achieve more → repeat forever.\n\nThe objective is to build enough control that you can pursue meaningful\ngoals while still experiencing life.\n\nA successful life can contain:\n\n-   ambitious projects\n-   difficult work\n-   learning\n-   failure\n-   friendships\n-   family\n-   travel\n-   fitness\n-   curiosity\n-   play\n-   relationships\n-   unexpected experiences\n\nYou should not reach the end of your life having achieved everything on\nyour task list but regretting everything you did not experience.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# 20. The person I want to help create\n\nThe ideal person shaped by this philosophy is someone who:\n\n-   controls their desire to doom-scroll\n-   plans their day before it begins\n-   reflects weekly on whether they are moving in the right direction\n-   does not wait for January 1 to change something\n-   tracks important behaviours until they become habits\n-   focuses on one demanding task at a time\n-   thinks ambitiously\n-   takes calculated risks\n-   is willing to look inexperienced while learning\n-   treats failure as information\n-   believes current ability is not necessarily the final ceiling\n-   uses technology deliberately\n-   adopts AI without surrendering independent thinking\n-   protects sleep and relationships\n-   continues to have experiences outside work\n-   pursues extraordinary goals without sacrificing the entirety of\n    their life\n\nUltimately, I want people to become **more capable, more self-directed,\nand more alive**.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# Core principles in one page\n\n1.  **Focus beats multitasking.**\n2.  **Dream bigger than your current evidence, but use reality to build\n    the strategy.**\n3.  **Extreme self-belief protects curiosity.**\n4.  **Novel problems require people willing to believe before proof\n    exists.**\n5.  **Execution matters more than having a beautiful strategy.**\n6.  **Failure is valuable when it teaches you how to improve.**\n7.  **Do not sacrifice your potential for short-term comfort and\n    validation.**\n8.  **Take calculated risks outside your comfort zone.**\n9.  **Your current ability is not necessarily your final ceiling.**\n10. **Use technology; do not let technology use you.**\n11. **Fight attention capture with environmental design and friction.**\n12. **Own your time.**\n13. **Productivity is control over your time, not maximum output.**\n14. **Ambition should create energy, not require constant motivational\n    stimulation.**\n15. **Do not glorify sleep deprivation or the 5 AM grind.**\n16. **Protect relationships, health, experiences, and curiosity.**\n17. **Start now rather than waiting for a symbolic reset date.**\n18. **Track behaviours until they become automatic.**\n19. **Use AI as an amplifier, never as a substitute for thinking.**\n20. **Do not become so focused on building the future that you forget to\n    live the present.**\n";
